{
  "an10.50:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 10.50 ",
  "an10.50:0.2": "5. Abuse ",
  "an10.50:0.3": "Fights ",
  "an10.50:1.1": "At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. ",
  "an10.50:1.2": "Now at that time, after the meal, on return from almsround, several mendicants sat together in the assembly hall. They were fighting, quarreling, and disputing, wounding each other with barbed words. ",
  "an10.50:2.1": "Then in the late afternoon, the Buddha came out of retreat and went to the assembly hall. He sat down on the seat spread out, ",
  "an10.50:2.2": "and addressed the mendicants: ",
  "an10.50:2.3": "“Mendicants, what were you sitting talking about just now? What conversation was left unfinished?” ",
  "an10.50:3.1": "“Sir, after the meal, on return from almsround, we sat together in the assembly hall, fighting, quarreling, and disputing, wounding each other with barbed words.” ",
  "an10.50:3.2": "“Mendicants, this is not appropriate for you gentlemen who have gone forth out of faith from the lay life to homelessness. ",
  "an10.50:4.1": "There are ten warm-hearted qualities that make for fondness and respect, conducing to inclusion, harmony, and unity, without dispute. ",
  "an10.50:4.2": "What ten? ",
  "an10.50:4.3": "Firstly, a mendicant is ethical, restrained in the monastic code, conducting themselves well and resorting for alms in suitable places. Seeing danger in the slightest fault, they keep the rules they’ve undertaken. ",
  "a